<section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Buyer guide</p><h1>Care and new development</h1><p class="lede">Keeping an agreed solution healthy and adding new capabilities are different kinds of work.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><article class="wrap prose"><p>By Wear Valley Digital · Updated <time datetime="2026-10-02">2 October 2026</time></p><h2>Start with the supported assets</h2><p>Ask which site, application and connections are covered. Identify who is responsible for hosting, domains, third-party accounts and updates. A support arrangement should make those responsibilities clear.</p><h2>Ask what recovery involves</h2><p>Ask what is backed up, how often, how long it is kept and how restoration is checked. A content backup alone may not recreate accounts, configuration or uploaded files. Agree the recovery process and any work outside the service.</p><h2>Separate response from resolution</h2><p>Confirm the support channel, operating hours and expected initial response. That response is different from fixing the problem: investigation may depend on access, a third-party service or the nature of the fault. Do not assume that every incident has the same resolution time.</p><h2>Give changes their own scope</h2><p>Adding a booking flow, connecting another system or redesigning a page changes what the product does. Describe the desired behaviour, agree its price and acceptance checks, and approve it before work starts.</p><h2>How WVD separates the work</h2><p>WVD Care covers maintenance, security updates, monitoring, appropriate backup and recovery, and fault investigation within the agreed scope. It is required with WVD-managed hosting and optional with customer-managed hosting.</p><p>New features, redesigns and routine content changes are scoped separately as Growth or project work. The quote sets out the supported assets, terms, exclusions and handover; this guide does not add service entitlements.</p><h2>Keep an exit route clear</h2><p>Agree how hosting, content, data and business-owned accounts will be handed over if the arrangement changes. Confirm any reusable software licences and third-party restrictions rather than assuming that every component has the same ownership terms.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/services/">Explore services and Care</a><a class="text-link" href="/insights/website-brief/">Prepare a website brief →</a></div><p><a href="/insights/">Back to buyer guides</a></p></article></section>
</BaseLayout>
